PH.unit({
  id:"m1", course:"mech", order:30, hours:13,
  title:"Mechanics 1: Kinematics",
  blurb:"Motion in 1D and 2D, built from derivatives and integrals. Constant-acceleration formulas are a special case, not the starting point.",
  needs:["prereq1","prereq2"],
  goals:[
    "Move between $x(t)$, $v(t)$, $a(t)$ using derivatives, integrals and initial conditions",
    "Know exactly when $v=v_0+at$ and friends are allowed (constant $a$ only)",
    "Read slopes and areas on motion graphs, including signed area vs distance traveled",
    "Solve projectile problems by treating $x$ and $y$ independently",
    "Solve $a=f(v)$ and $a=f(x)$ problems with separation of variables",
    "Work with vector notation: differentiate $\\vec r(t)$ componentwise, find speed, and split $\\vec a$ into parts parallel and perpendicular to $\\vec v$",
    "Derive and use the trajectory equation $y(x)$; solve launches from a height, impact speed and angle, and projectiles on an incline",
    "Derive the best launch angle (level ground, from a height, up a slope) and use relative motion for chase and river problems"
  ],
  lessons:[
  {id:"l1", title:"Position, velocity, acceleration", videos:[{title:"Search: AP Physics C position velocity acceleration derivatives integrals",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AP+Physics+C+position+velocity+acceleration+derivatives+integrals",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<p>Everything in kinematics is calculus on one function, the position $x(t)$ (or vector $\vec r(t)$):</p>
$$v=\frac{dx}{dt},\qquad a=\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{d^2x}{dt^2},\qquad x(t)=x_0+\int_0^t v\,dt',\qquad v(t)=v_0+\int_0^t a\,dt'$$
<p>The integrals need <b>initial conditions</b> ($x_0$, $v_0$). On the AP, forgetting the constant of integration is the most common lost point in a "derive" question.</p>
<div class="box key"><b>Signs matter.</b> Velocity is signed (direction). Speed is $|v|$. A particle can be speeding up with negative acceleration if its velocity is also negative.</div>
<p><b>Displacement vs distance.</b> Displacement is $\Delta x=\int v\,dt$ (signed). Distance traveled is $\int |v|\,dt$: split the integral wherever $v$ changes sign.</p>
<div class="box trap"><b>Trap:</b> "At rest" means $v=0$ <i>now</i>, not $a=0$. At the top of a throw, $v=0$ but $a=-g$.</div>`},
  {id:"l2", title:"Constant acceleration: where the formulas come from", videos:[{title:"Search: AP Physics C constant acceleration derivation calculus kinematics",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AP+Physics+C+constant+acceleration+derivation+calculus+kinematics",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<p>If $a$ is a constant, integrate once and twice:</p>
$$v=v_0+at,\qquad x=x_0+v_0t+\tfrac12at^2$$
<p>Eliminating $t$ gives $v^2=v_0^2+2a(x-x_0)$. These hold <b>only</b> for constant $a$. If $a$ depends on $t$, $x$ or $v$, go back to integrals.</p>
<p>Useful chain-rule trick for $a$ as a function of position:</p>
$$a=\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{dv}{dx}\frac{dx}{dt}=v\frac{dv}{dx}\quad\Rightarrow\quad \int v\,dv=\int a\,dx$$
<p>This is the first version of the work-energy theorem you will meet in Unit 3.</p>
<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 340 190" width="340" role="img" aria-label="v versus t graph, a line rising from v0 to v with the area underneath shaded">
<line x1="40" y1="160" x2="320" y2="160" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="160" x2="40" y2="15" stroke="currentColor"/>
<polygon points="40,160 40,100 280,40 280,160" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity=".12"/>
<line x1="40" y1="100" x2="280" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"/>
<text x="300" y="178">t</text><text x="18" y="22">v</text><text x="6" y="104">v₀</text><text x="285" y="35">v</text><text x="270" y="178">t</text>
<text x="120" y="135">area = Δx</text><text x="150" y="62">slope = a</text></svg>`},
  {id:"l3", title:"Reading motion graphs", videos:[{title:"Search: AP Physics C motion graphs slope area x v a",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AP+Physics+C+motion+graphs+slope+area+x+v+a",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<table><tr><th>Graph</th><th>Slope gives</th><th>Area under gives</th></tr>
<tr><td>$x$ vs $t$</td><td>$v$</td><td>(nothing useful)</td></tr>
<tr><td>$v$ vs $t$</td><td>$a$</td><td>$\Delta x$</td></tr>
<tr><td>$a$ vs $t$</td><td>"jerk"</td><td>$\Delta v$</td></tr></table>
<p>Curvature of $x(t)$: concave up means $a>0$, concave down means $a<0$. An inflection point is where $a$ changes sign. Areas <b>below</b> the axis are negative displacement.</p>
<p>The AP often asks you to sketch $v(t)$ or $a(t)$ from a description, or to go from one graph to another. Practice: label the sign and rough size of slope in each interval before drawing.</p>`},
  {id:"l4", title:"Projectiles and variable acceleration", videos:[{title:"Search: AP Physics C projectile motion and acceleration depends on velocity separation of variables",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AP+Physics+C+projectile+motion+and+acceleration+depends+on+velocity+separation+of+variables",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<p><b>Projectiles (no air resistance).</b> Horizontal and vertical motion are independent. With launch speed $v_0$ at angle $\theta$ above horizontal and $g$ pointing down:</p>
$$v_x=v_0\cos\theta,\qquad v_y=v_0\sin\theta-gt,\qquad x=v_0\cos\theta\,t,\qquad y=v_0\sin\theta\,t-\tfrac12gt^2$$
<p>The one thing the two directions share is the time $t$. For a launch and landing at the same height: time of flight $T=2v_0\sin\theta/g$, range $R=v_0^2\sin2\theta/g$, max range at $45^\circ$.</p>
<p><b>Variable acceleration.</b> Example: $a=-kv$ (linear drag). Separate and integrate:</p>
$$\frac{dv}{dt}=-kv\Rightarrow\int_{v_0}^{v}\frac{dv'}{v'}=-k\int_0^t dt'\Rightarrow v=v_0e^{-kt}$$
<p>Then $x=\int v\,dt=\frac{v_0}{k}(1-e^{-kt})$, so the object approaches a finite stopping distance $v_0/k$ but never quite stops in finite time. You will see this exact structure again with terminal velocity (Unit 2), the RC circuit (E&amp;M 3) and the RL circuit.</p>`},
  {id:"l5", title:"Vector notation and 2D kinematics with calculus", videos:[{title:"Search: AP Physics C position velocity acceleration vectors unit vectors i j derivative",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AP+Physics+C+position+velocity+acceleration+vectors+unit+vectors+derivative",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<p>In two dimensions the position is a <b>vector</b>: $\vec r(t)=x(t)\,\hat\imath+y(t)\,\hat\jmath$, where $\hat\imath$ and $\hat\jmath$ are fixed unit vectors (length 1) along $x$ and $y$. Because $\hat\imath,\hat\jmath$ never change, you differentiate and integrate <b>component by component</b>:</p>
$$\vec v=\frac{d\vec r}{dt}=\dot x\,\hat\imath+\dot y\,\hat\jmath,\qquad \vec a=\frac{d\vec v}{dt}=\ddot x\,\hat\imath+\ddot y\,\hat\jmath$$
<p>Notation: a dot over a symbol means $d/dt$; $\vec v=\langle v_x,v_y\rangle$ is an equivalent way to write components. The <b>speed</b> is the length of the velocity vector, $v=|\vec v|=\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}$. The velocity vector is always <b>tangent to the path</b>, and its direction angle satisfies $\tan\beta=v_y/v_x$.</p>
<p><b>Worked pattern.</b> $\vec r=(t^2)\hat\imath+(3t)\hat\jmath$ gives $\vec v=2t\,\hat\imath+3\,\hat\jmath$, $\vec a=2\,\hat\imath$. The acceleration points along $+x$ only, yet the path is a parabola, because $y=3t$ gives $t=y/3$ and $x=y^2/9$ (elimination of $t$ is how you get the path shape).</p>
<div class="box key"><b>What changes the speed?</b> Since $v^2=\vec v\cdot\vec v$, differentiate: $2v\,\dfrac{dv}{dt}=2\,\vec v\cdot\vec a$, so
$$\frac{d(\text{speed})}{dt}=\frac{\vec v\cdot\vec a}{|\vec v|}=a_\parallel$$
the component of $\vec a$ <i>along</i> the velocity. If $\vec a\cdot\vec v&gt;0$ the particle speeds up, if $&lt;0$ it slows down, and if $\vec a\perp\vec v$ the speed is momentarily constant (an extremum of speed, when it happens at an instant). The other component, $a_\perp$ (perpendicular to $\vec v$), does not change speed; it only <i>turns</i> the velocity, with $a_\perp=v^2/\rho$ where $\rho$ is the radius of curvature of the path at that point.</div>
<p>So $\vec a=a_\parallel\hat t+a_\perp\hat n$, with $\hat t=\vec v/|\vec v|$ tangent and $\hat n$ perpendicular. In practice: $a_\parallel=\vec a\cdot\vec v/|\vec v|$ and $a_\perp=\sqrt{|\vec a|^2-a_\parallel^2}$ (or $|\vec a\times\vec v|/|\vec v|$).</p>
<div class="box trap"><b>Trap:</b> $\left|\dfrac{d\vec v}{dt}\right|\neq\dfrac{d|\vec v|}{dt}$. The magnitude of the acceleration vector is not the rate of change of speed. In uniform circular motion the speed is constant ($d|\vec v|/dt=0$) but $|\vec a|\ne0$.</div>
<p><b>Uniform circular motion from $\vec r(t)$.</b> A particle on a circle of radius $R$ at constant angular speed $\omega$ (angle $\theta=\omega t$):</p>
$$\vec r=R(\cos\omega t\,\hat\imath+\sin\omega t\,\hat\jmath),\quad \vec v=R\omega(-\sin\omega t\,\hat\imath+\cos\omega t\,\hat\jmath),\quad \vec a=-R\omega^2(\cos\omega t\,\hat\imath+\sin\omega t\,\hat\jmath)=-\omega^2\vec r$$
<p>So $v=R\omega$ (constant), $\vec v\cdot\vec r=0$ (velocity is tangent), and $\vec a=-\omega^2\vec r$ points toward the center with magnitude $\omega^2R=v^2/R$. This is the centripetal acceleration you will use in Mechanics 2; here it falls out of two derivatives.</p>`},
  {id:"l6", title:"Projectile trajectory y(x), launch from a height, impact velocity", videos:[{title:"Walter Lewin, MIT 8.01 Lecture 4: 3D kinematics, free-falling frames (projectiles, monkey and gun)",url:"https://youtu.be/k6aJyOHTDYM",src:"Walter Lewin lectures",note:"link found in a course listing and matched by title, not watched; the golf-ball gun and monkey demo is the famous part"},{title:"AP Physics C Projectile Motion Example 2 FRQ",url:"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PFUqW4un2LQ",src:"YouTube (AP Physics C)",note:"found in search results by title; channel not confirmed"}], html:String.raw`
<p><b>The trajectory equation.</b> From $x=v_0\cos\theta\,t$ solve $t=x/(v_0\cos\theta)$ and substitute into $y=v_0\sin\theta\,t-\tfrac12gt^2$:</p>
$$\boxed{\,y=x\tan\theta-\frac{g\,x^2}{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta}\,}$$
<p>This is a <b>parabola</b> opening downward: $y$ is quadratic in $x$. It has a nicer form using the level-ground range $R=v_0^2\sin2\theta/g$. Since $\dfrac{g}{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta}=\dfrac{\tan\theta}{R}$,</p>
$$y=x\tan\theta\left(1-\frac{x}{R}\right)$$
<p>Read it off: zeros at $x=0$ and $x=R$; by symmetry the apex is at $x=R/2$ with height $H=\tfrac14R\tan\theta$; the initial slope is $\tan\theta$. Because the path is a parabola, the ball is at the same height at $x=R/2\pm d$ with the same speed (symmetric up and down legs).</p>
<div class="box key"><b>Slope of the path = direction of velocity.</b> $dy/dx=\dfrac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}=\dfrac{v_y}{v_x}$. Differentiating the trajectory equation gives $\tan\beta$ at any point without ever mentioning time. Use $y(x)$ when the question is about <i>where</i> (a wall, a window, a target); use $x(t),y(t)$ when it is about <i>when</i>.</div>
<p><b>Launch from a height $h$.</b> Put the origin on the ground below the launch point:</p>
$$y=h+x\tan\theta-\frac{g x^2}{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta},\qquad\text{or in time: }y=h+v_0\sin\theta\,t-\tfrac12gt^2$$
<p>To find the landing, set $y=0$: this is a quadratic. Take the <b>positive</b> root for $t$ (the negative root is the time at which the parabola, extended backward, would have passed ground level). Then $x_{land}=v_0\cos\theta\,t$. For a horizontal launch ($\theta=0$): $y=h-gx^2/2v_0^2$, $t=\sqrt{2h/g}$, $x_{land}=v_0\sqrt{2h/g}$.</p>
<p><b>Velocity at impact.</b> $v_x$ never changes. Get $v_y$ from $v_y=v_0\sin\theta-gt$ at the landing time, or without solving for $t$ using the result of Lesson 2, $v\,dv=a\,dy$ with $a=-g$:</p>
$$v^2=v_0^2-2g(y-y_0)\ \Rightarrow\ v_{impact}=\sqrt{v_0^2+2gh}$$
<p>This is just the work-energy theorem of Unit 3 in disguise. It says that the <b>impact speed depends only on $v_0$ and $h$, not on the launch angle</b>: throwing a ball up, down or sideways from a cliff at the same speed gives the same impact speed. The impact <b>angle</b> does depend on the launch angle: $\tan\beta=|v_y|/v_x$ with $v_x=v_0\cos\theta$ and $|v_y|=\sqrt{v_{impact}^2-v_x^2}$.</p>
<div class="box trap"><b>Trap:</b> the speed at the highest point is $v_0\cos\theta$, not zero. Only $v_y$ is zero there. Also, do not use $R=v_0^2\sin2\theta/g$ or $T=2v_0\sin\theta/g$ when the landing is at a different level: those assume launch and landing at the same height.</div>`},
  {id:"l7", title:"Projectile on an incline", videos:[{title:"Search: projectile motion on an incline tilted axes range up the slope",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=projectile+motion+on+an+incline+tilted+axes+range+up+the+slope",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<p>A ball is launched from a point on a plane inclined at angle $\varphi$ and lands on the plane. Two clean methods; each is useful, and you should check one against the other.</p>
<p><b>Method 1: tilted axes.</b> Choose $x'$ <i>along</i> the slope (up-slope positive) and $y'$ <i>perpendicular</i> to it. Gravity has components $-g\sin\varphi$ along $x'$ and $-g\cos\varphi$ along $y'$, both constant. Let the launch be at angle $\alpha$ above the <i>slope surface</i> (so $\alpha=\theta-\varphi$ if $\theta$ is measured from the horizontal):</p>
$$v_{x'0}=v_0\cos\alpha,\quad v_{y'0}=v_0\sin\alpha,\quad a_{x'}=-g\sin\varphi,\quad a_{y'}=-g\cos\varphi$$
<p>The ball is back on the slope when $y'=0$: $T=\dfrac{2v_0\sin\alpha}{g\cos\varphi}$. The range <i>along the slope</i> is</p>
$$R=v_0\cos\alpha\,T-\tfrac12g\sin\varphi\,T^2=\frac{2v_0^2\sin\alpha\cos(\alpha+\varphi)}{g\cos^2\varphi}$$
<p>(the last step uses $\cos\alpha\cos\varphi-\sin\alpha\sin\varphi=\cos(\alpha+\varphi)$). For $\varphi=0$ it reduces to $v_0^2\sin2\alpha/g$.</p>
<p><b>Method 2: keep horizontal and vertical axes.</b> The trajectory is $y=x\tan\theta-\dfrac{gx^2}{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta}$ and the slope surface is the line $y=x\tan\varphi$. They meet at $x=0$ and at the second root, where $x\tan\theta-\tan\varphi\,x=\dfrac{gx^2}{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta}$:</p>
$$x_{hit}=\frac{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta\,(\tan\theta-\tan\varphi)}{g},\qquad R=\frac{x_{hit}}{\cos\varphi}$$
<p><b>Angle for maximum range up the slope.</b> Use $\sin\alpha\cos(\alpha+\varphi)=\tfrac12[\sin(2\alpha+\varphi)-\sin\varphi]$. Only the first term depends on $\alpha$, and it is largest when $2\alpha+\varphi=90^\circ$:</p>
$$\alpha=45^\circ-\frac\varphi2,\qquad \theta=\alpha+\varphi=45^\circ+\frac\varphi2,\qquad R_{max}=\frac{v_0^2(1-\sin\varphi)}{g\cos^2\varphi}=\frac{v_0^2}{g(1+\sin\varphi)}$$
<p>Interpretation: the best launch direction bisects the angle between the slope surface and the vertical. Down the slope replace $\varphi\to-\varphi$: $R_{max}=v_0^2/[g(1-\sin\varphi)]$, larger than on level ground.</p>
<div class="box trap"><b>Trap:</b> mixing the two coordinate systems. If you use tilted axes, <i>all</i> of $v_0$, $g$ and the displacement must be resolved into slope components. The range from Method 1 is measured <i>along the slope</i>; the range from Method 2 (before dividing by $\cos\varphi$) is <i>horizontal</i>.</div>`},
  {id:"l8", title:"Extension: deriving the best launch angle (level ground and from a height)", extra:true, videos:[{title:"Search: projectile maximum range angle from a cliff derivation",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=projectile+maximum+range+angle+from+a+cliff+derivation+calculus",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<p><b>Level ground, by calculus.</b> $R(\theta)=\dfrac{v_0^2\sin2\theta}{g}$. Then $\dfrac{dR}{d\theta}=\dfrac{2v_0^2\cos2\theta}{g}=0\Rightarrow2\theta=90^\circ$, $\theta=45^\circ$, and $d^2R/d\theta^2=-4v_0^2\sin2\theta/g&lt;0$ confirms a maximum with $R_{max}=v_0^2/g$.</p>
<p><b>Complementary angles.</b> $\sin2\theta=\sin(180^\circ-2\theta)=\sin2(90^\circ-\theta)$, so $\theta$ and $90^\circ-\theta$ give the <i>same range</i> (20 and 70 degrees, say), but the steeper one stays up longer and rises higher: $T\propto\sin\theta$, $H\propto\sin^2\theta$.</p>
<p><b>From a height $h$ (the angle is below $45^\circ$).</b> Direct differentiation of $R(\theta)$ is messy because of the square root. A cleaner route: for a given $v_0$ and a target at horizontal distance $x$ on the ground, write $u=\tan\theta$ and $1/\cos^2\theta=1+u^2$ in the trajectory:</p>
$$0=h+xu-\frac{gx^2}{2v_0^2}(1+u^2)\ \Rightarrow\ \frac{gx^2}{2v_0^2}\,u^2-x\,u+\left(\frac{gx^2}{2v_0^2}-h\right)=0$$
<p>This is a quadratic in $u$. A real launch angle exists only if its discriminant is non-negative: $x^2-4\cdot\dfrac{gx^2}{2v_0^2}\left(\dfrac{gx^2}{2v_0^2}-h\right)\ge0$, which simplifies to</p>
$$x^2\le\frac{v_0^2}{g^2}\left(v_0^2+2gh\right)\quad\Rightarrow\quad R_{max}=\frac{v_0}{g}\sqrt{v_0^2+2gh}$$
<p>At the boundary the discriminant is zero, so $u=\dfrac{x}{2(gx^2/2v_0^2)}=\dfrac{v_0^2}{gx}$, giving</p>
$$\tan\theta_{best}=\frac{v_0}{\sqrt{v_0^2+2gh}}&lt;1$$
<p>For $h=0$ this is $\tan\theta=1$, $45^\circ$. For $h>0$ the angle is smaller: the extra height already buys flight time, so it pays to aim flatter. (Check with $v_0=20$ m/s, $h=15$ m, $g=10$: $\theta_{best}=37.1^\circ$, $R_{max}=52.9$ m, only slightly better than the $51.96$ m of a $30^\circ$ launch.)</p>
<p><b>Obstacles and the "monkey and hunter".</b> To clear a wall of height $y_w$ at distance $x_w$, require $y(x_w)\ge y_w$ using the trajectory equation (and check whether the ball is still rising or already falling there if it matters). In the classic demonstration, a monkey lets go of a branch at the instant a dart is fired straight at it. In the frame where both fall freely, the dart moves in a straight line at constant velocity (the $-\tfrac12gt^2$ drop cancels for both), so the dart <b>always hits</b> the monkey, provided it reaches the monkey's line before hitting the ground, regardless of the dart's speed.</p>`},
  {id:"l9", title:"Extension: relative motion and chase problems", extra:true, videos:[{title:"Search: relative velocity boat river rain Galilean transformation physics",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=relative+velocity+boat+river+crossing+rain+physics+calculus",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<p><b>Relative velocity.</b> Let $\vec v_{A/B}$ be the velocity of $A$ as measured by an observer who is at rest in frame $B$. For frames moving at a constant velocity relative to each other (Galilean transformation):</p>
$$\vec v_{A/C}=\vec v_{A/B}+\vec v_{B/C},\qquad \vec v_{A/B}=\vec v_A-\vec v_B$$
<p>and if frame $B$ moves at constant velocity, $A$ has the <i>same acceleration</i> in both frames ($\vec a_{A/B}=\vec a_A$). This is why Newton's laws look the same in all constant-velocity frames (Unit 2).</p>
<p><b>Boat and river:</b> $\vec v_{boat/ground}=\vec v_{boat/water}+\vec v_{water/ground}$. Crossing time depends only on the boat's velocity component <i>perpendicular</i> to the banks. <b>Rain on a car:</b> the rain's velocity relative to the car is $\vec v_{rain}-\vec v_{car}$, which tilts the streaks toward the front.</p>
<p><b>Two projectiles.</b> Two balls launched at the same time from the same point with different velocities have $\vec r_2-\vec r_1=(\vec v_{02}-\vec v_{01})t$ since the $-\tfrac12gt^2\hat\jmath$ terms cancel. In the free-falling frame both move in straight lines at constant velocity and their separation grows <i>linearly</i> in time.</p>
<p><b>Chase and meeting problems.</b> Write $x_A(t)$ and $x_B(t)$ with a common origin and clock, set $x_A=x_B$ for the meeting time (the quadratic usually has two roots; the physically sensible one, often the later one, is the answer), and set $v_A=v_B$ for the instant of <i>maximum or minimum separation</i> (separation $s(t)$ has $ds/dt=v_A-v_B=0$). <b>Multi-phase motion</b>: treat each phase with its own constant $a$, using the end of one phase as the initial conditions of the next (for example a reaction time at constant speed, then braking).</p>`},
  {id:"l10", title:"Extension: acceleration that depends on velocity, beyond linear drag", extra:true, videos:[{title:"Search: AP Physics C a = -kv^2 separation of variables velocity position",url:"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=AP+Physics+C+quadratic+drag+a%3D-kv%5E2+separation+of+variables",src:"YouTube search",note:"search link, not a vetted video"}], html:String.raw`
<p>Three equivalent ways to write the same motion, and which to pick:</p>
<table><tr><th>You want</th><th>Use</th></tr>
<tr><td>$v(t)$</td><td>$a=dv/dt$, separate $v$ and $t$</td></tr>
<tr><td>$v(x)$</td><td>$a=v\,dv/dx$, separate $v$ and $x$</td></tr>
<tr><td>$x(t)$</td><td>integrate $v(t)$</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Quadratic drag, $a=-kv^2$.</b> Here $k$ has units 1/m.</p>
$$\frac{dv}{v^2}=-k\,dt\ \Rightarrow\ \frac1v-\frac1{v_0}=kt\ \Rightarrow\ v=\frac{v_0}{1+kv_0t},\qquad x=\frac1k\ln(1+kv_0t)$$
<p>Using $v\,dv/dx=-kv^2$ instead: $dv/v=-k\,dx$, so $v=v_0e^{-kx}$. Now compare the two drag laws, which behave very differently:</p>
<table><tr><th></th><th>$a=-kv$</th><th>$a=-kv^2$</th></tr>
<tr><td>$v(t)$</td><td>$v_0e^{-kt}$</td><td>$v_0/(1+kv_0t)$ (slower, power-law decay)</td></tr>
<tr><td>$v(x)$</td><td>$v_0-kx$ (linear)</td><td>$v_0e^{-kx}$</td></tr>
<tr><td>total distance</td><td>finite, $v_0/k$</td><td>infinite (logarithm diverges)</td></tr>
<tr><td>total time</td><td>infinite</td><td>infinite</td></tr></table>
<p>Physical reason: with $a\propto v^2$ the drag becomes negligible at low speed, so the object coasts forever; with $a\propto v$ the drag fades only proportionally, and the stopping distance converges.</p>
<p><b>Projectile with linear drag</b> (for the curious): $x$ and $y$ separate because the drag acts on each velocity component. $\dot v_x=-kv_x$ gives $v_x=v_{0x}e^{-kt}$; $\dot v_y=-g-kv_y$ gives $v_y=(v_{0y}+g/k)e^{-kt}-g/k$, which tends to the terminal value $-g/k$. The trajectory is then no longer a parabola and is not symmetric: it falls more steeply than it rises. Cases without a closed form can be integrated step by step numerically (Euler's method: $v\leftarrow v+a\,\Delta t$, $x\leftarrow x+v\,\Delta t$), which is how a spreadsheet or program solves any $a(v,t,x)$.</p>`}
  ],
  examples:[
  {id:"x1", title:"Distance vs displacement", problem:String.raw`<p>A particle has $x(t)=t^3-6t^2+9t$ (SI units). Find the displacement and the total distance traveled from $t=0$ to $t=4$ s.</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>$v=3t^2-12t+9=3(t-1)(t-3)$, so $v=0$ at $t=1,3$ and the particle turns around there.</p>
<p>$x(0)=0$, $x(1)=4$, $x(3)=0$, $x(4)=4$. Displacement $=4$ m. Distance $=|4-0|+|0-4|+|4-0|=12$ m.</p>`},
  {id:"x2", title:"Velocity as a function of position", problem:String.raw`<p>A block has acceleration $a=-4x$ (SI) and is at $x=0.5$ m moving at $v=0$. Find its speed at $x=0$.</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>Use $v\,dv=a\,dx$: $\int_0^{v}v'\,dv'=\int_{0.5}^{0}(-4x)\,dx$, so $\tfrac12v^2=-2x^2\Big|_{0.5}^{0}=0+2(0.25)=0.5$. Thus $v^2=1$, $|v|=1$ m/s.</p><p>Note you could not use $v^2=v_0^2+2a\Delta x$ here because $a$ is not constant.</p>`},
  {id:"x3", title:"Projectile hitting a target", problem:String.raw`<p>A ball is launched from the ground at $v_0=20$ m/s, $\theta=30^\circ$ (use $g=10$ m/s²). Find the time of flight, the range, and the maximum height.</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>$v_{0y}=10$ m/s, $v_{0x}=10\sqrt3\approx17.3$ m/s. Time of flight $T=2(10)/10=2$ s. Range $=17.3\times2\approx34.6$ m. Max height: $v_y=0$ at $t=1$ s, $y=10(1)-5(1)^2=5$ m.</p>`},
  {id:"x4", title:"Using the trajectory equation: clearing a wall", problem:String.raw`<p>A ball is launched from level ground with $v_0=25$ m/s at an angle with $\tan\theta=3/4$ (so $\cos\theta=0.8$, $\sin\theta=0.6$). Use $g=10$ m/s². (a) Derive $y(x)$ and find the range and maximum height. (b) Does the ball clear an $8$ m wall that stands $40$ m from the launch point? Is the ball rising or falling as it passes the wall?</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) $v_{0x}=20$, $v_{0y}=15$ m/s. $y=x\tan\theta-\dfrac{gx^2}{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta}=0.75x-\dfrac{10x^2}{2(625)(0.64)}=0.75x-\dfrac{x^2}{80}$. Zeros: $x=0$ and $x=60$ m, so $R=60$ m (check: $v_0^2\sin2\theta/g=625(0.96)/10=60$). The apex is at $x=30$ m: $H=0.75(30)-900/80=22.5-11.25=11.25$ m (check: $v_{0y}^2/2g=225/20=11.25$).</p>
<p>(b) $y(40)=30-1600/80=10$ m. This exceeds 8 m, so the ball clears the wall by 2 m. The slope there is $dy/dx=0.75-2x/80=0.75-1=-0.25$, negative, so the ball is falling (check in time: $t=40/20=2$ s, $v_y=15-20=-5$ m/s, and $v_y/v_x=-5/20=-0.25$ ✓).</p>`},
  {id:"x5", title:"Launch from a cliff: landing, impact speed and angle", problem:String.raw`<p>A ball is thrown from the edge of a $15$ m cliff with speed $20$ m/s at $30^\circ$ <i>above</i> the horizontal (use $g=10$ m/s²). Find (a) the time of flight, (b) the horizontal distance from the base of the cliff, (c) the impact speed, two ways, and (d) the angle below the horizontal of the velocity at impact.</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) $v_{0x}=20\cos30^\circ=17.32$ m/s, $v_{0y}=10$ m/s. With the origin at the base, $0=15+10t-5t^2\Rightarrow t^2-2t-3=0\Rightarrow(t-3)(t+1)=0$. Take $t=3$ s (reject $-1$ s).</p>
<p>(b) $x=17.32(3)=52.0$ m.</p>
<p>(c) By components: $v_y=10-10(3)=-20$ m/s, so $v=\sqrt{17.32^2+20^2}=\sqrt{700}=26.5$ m/s. By the shortcut: $v=\sqrt{v_0^2+2gh}=\sqrt{400+300}=26.5$ m/s ✓. No time needed.</p>
<p>(d) $\tan\beta=20/17.32\Rightarrow\beta=49.1^\circ$ below horizontal. Same speed, different angle: a ball thrown $30^\circ$ <i>below</i> the horizontal at 20 m/s has the identical impact velocity (the upward throw falls back through the cliff-top height at 20 m/s, $30^\circ$ below horizontal), while a horizontal throw at 20 m/s also lands at $26.5$ m/s but at the shallower $\beta=\arctan(17.3/20)=40.9^\circ$.</p>`},
  {id:"x6", title:"2D motion from r(t): speed, a parallel and a perpendicular", problem:String.raw`<p>A particle has position $\vec r(t)=2t\,\hat\imath+(t^2-4t)\,\hat\jmath$ (SI units). (a) Find $\vec v$ and $\vec a$. (b) At what time is the speed least, and what is it? (c) At $t=0$, find the components of $\vec a$ parallel and perpendicular to $\vec v$. Is the particle speeding up or slowing down there?</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) $\vec v=2\,\hat\imath+(2t-4)\,\hat\jmath$, $\vec a=2\,\hat\jmath$ (constant).</p>
<p>(b) $v^2=4+(2t-4)^2$ is least when $2t-4=0$, i.e. $t=2$ s, where $v=2$ m/s. At that instant $\vec v=2\hat\imath\perp\vec a=2\hat\jmath$, as the rule $d(\text{speed})/dt=\vec a\cdot\vec v/|\vec v|=0$ predicts.</p>
<p>(c) At $t=0$: $\vec v=2\hat\imath-4\hat\jmath$, $|\vec v|=\sqrt{20}=4.47$ m/s. $a_\parallel=\vec a\cdot\vec v/|\vec v|=(0\cdot2+2\cdot(-4))/4.47=-1.79$ m/s². $a_\perp=\sqrt{|\vec a|^2-a_\parallel^2}=\sqrt{4-3.2}=0.894$ m/s² (also $|\vec a\times\vec v|/|\vec v|=4/4.47$ ✓). Since $a_\parallel&lt;0$ the particle is slowing down at $t=0$ even though $|\vec a|=2$ m/s² is constant. The radius of curvature there is $\rho=v^2/a_\perp=20/0.894=22.4$ m.</p>`},
  {id:"x7", title:"Projectile on an incline", problem:String.raw`<p>A ball is launched from the bottom of a $30^\circ$ incline with speed $20$ m/s at $45^\circ$ above the horizontal, in the vertical plane containing the line of steepest slope, up the incline (use $g=10$ m/s²). Find where it lands (distance along the slope) using both methods. Compare with the greatest possible range up this slope.</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p><b>Method 1 (tilted axes).</b> The launch is $\alpha=45^\circ-30^\circ=15^\circ$ above the slope. $v_{x'0}=20\cos15^\circ=19.32$, $v_{y'0}=20\sin15^\circ=5.176$ m/s, $a_{x'}=-g\sin30^\circ=-5$, $a_{y'}=-g\cos30^\circ=-8.66$ m/s². Time on the slope: $T=2(5.176)/8.66=1.195$ s. Distance along the slope: $19.32(1.195)-\tfrac12(5)(1.195)^2=23.09-3.57=19.52$ m.</p>
<p><b>Method 2 (horizontal axes).</b> $y=x\tan45^\circ-\dfrac{10x^2}{2(400)(0.5)}=x-\dfrac{x^2}{40}$. Slope surface: $y=x\tan30^\circ=0.5774x$. Setting equal: $x(1-0.5774)=x^2/40\Rightarrow x=40(0.4226)=16.91$ m horizontally, so $R=16.91/\cos30^\circ=19.52$ m ✓.</p>
<p><b>Compare.</b> $R_{max}=v_0^2/[g(1+\sin\varphi)]=400/(10\cdot1.5)=26.7$ m, at $\theta=45^\circ+15^\circ=60^\circ$. The $45^\circ$ launch gets only 73% of that. (On level ground, $45^\circ$ would be optimal, so the incline shifts the best angle <i>toward the steeper side</i>.)</p>`},
  {id:"x8", title:"Chase and multi-phase motion", problem:String.raw`<p>(a) A car moves at a constant $20$ m/s. As it passes a stationary police car, the police car starts from rest with constant acceleration $4$ m/s². When and where does the police car catch up, and what is the greatest gap between them before that? (b) A driver traveling at $25$ m/s sees an obstacle and brakes with deceleration $7.5$ m/s², after a reaction time of $0.6$ s during which the speed does not change. Find the total stopping distance.</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) $x_A=20t$, $x_P=\tfrac12(4)t^2=2t^2$. Equal when $20t=2t^2$: $t=0$ (start) or $t=10$ s. Position $=200$ m. The police car's speed then is $4(10)=40$ m/s, twice the car's. The gap $s=20t-2t^2$ is greatest when $ds/dt=20-4t=0$ ($v_A=v_P$): $t=5$ s, $s_{max}=100-50=50$ m.</p>
<p>(b) Reaction phase: $25(0.6)=15$ m. Braking phase: $v^2=v_0^2+2a\Delta x\Rightarrow0=625-2(7.5)\Delta x\Rightarrow\Delta x=41.7$ m. Total $\approx56.7$ m. (The reaction phase is a sizeable 26% of the distance.)</p>`},
  {id:"x9", title:"Quadratic drag: a = -k v squared", problem:String.raw`<p>A glider on a track has acceleration $a=-kv^2$ with $k=0.05$ m$^{-1}$ and initial velocity $v_0=20$ m/s at $x=0$. Find $v(t)$, $x(t)$ and $v(x)$. How far has it gone when its speed is $5$ m/s, and how long did that take? How far does it travel in total?</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>$\dfrac{dv}{dt}=-kv^2\Rightarrow\int_{v_0}^v\dfrac{dv'}{v'^2}=-k\int_0^tdt'\Rightarrow\dfrac1{v_0}-\dfrac1v=-kt\Rightarrow v=\dfrac{v_0}{1+kv_0t}=\dfrac{20}{1+t}$ (SI). Position: $x=\int_0^tv\,dt'=\dfrac1k\ln(1+kv_0t)=20\ln(1+t)$. From $a=v\,dv/dx=-kv^2$: $dv/v=-k\,dx$, so $v=v_0e^{-kx}$.</p>
<p>$v=5$ m/s: $1+t=4$, $t=3$ s; $x=20\ln4=27.7$ m. Check with $v(x)$: $20e^{-0.05(27.7)}=20e^{-1.386}=5.0$ ✓. Total distance: as $t\to\infty$, $\ln(1+t)\to\infty$; the glider never stops and the distance is unbounded. Compare with $a=-kv$, where the distance is finite.</p>`},
  {id:"x10", title:"From an a(t) graph to v(t) and x(t)", problem:String.raw`<p>A cart starts from rest at $x=0$. Its acceleration is $a=+2$ m/s² for $0\le t\le4$ s, $a=0$ for $4\le t\le10$ s, and $a=-3$ m/s² for $10\le t\le14$ s. Find $v$ and $x$ at $t=4,10,14$ s; the time at which it reverses direction; and the total distance traveled.</p>
<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 330 150" width="330" role="img" aria-label="a versus t: a equals 2 from 0 to 4 s, zero from 4 to 10 s, and minus 3 from 10 to 14 s"><line x1="30" y1="70" x2="320" y2="70" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="30" y1="10" x2="30" y2="140" stroke="currentColor"/><polyline points="30,70 30,40 94,40 94,70 254,70 254,115 318,115" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="16" y="44">2</text><text x="10" y="119">-3</text><text x="90" y="85">4</text><text x="250" y="62">10</text><text x="312" y="85">14</text><text x="12" y="15">a</text><text x="320" y="62">t</text></svg>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>$\Delta v$ = area under $a(t)$. $v(4)=2(4)=8$ m/s. $v(10)=8+0=8$ m/s. $v(14)=8-3(4)=-4$ m/s. So $v$ changes sign inside the last interval: $8-3(t-10)=0\Rightarrow t=12.67$ s, where it reverses.</p>
<p>$\Delta x$ = area under $v(t)$. $x(4)=\tfrac12(8)(4)=16$ m. $x(10)=16+8(6)=64$ m. From 10 to 12.67 s: triangle $\tfrac12(8)(2.67)=10.67$ m, so $x(12.67)=74.67$ m (the farthest point). From 12.67 to 14 s: $\tfrac12(-4)(1.33)=-2.67$ m, so $x(14)=72.0$ m.</p>
<p>Distance traveled $=74.67+2.67=77.3$ m, while the displacement is $72.0$ m. Sketch of $x(t)$: parabola curving up ($a>0$), then a straight line of slope 8, then a downward-curving arc with a maximum at 12.67 s.</p>`},
  {id:"x11", title:"Relative motion: river crossing and rain", problem:String.raw`<p>(a) A boat can move at $5$ m/s relative to still water. It crosses a river $60$ m wide flowing at $3$ m/s. Find the crossing time and the downstream drift if it aims straight across, and the heading and crossing time needed to land directly opposite. (b) Rain falls vertically at $6$ m/s. A car moves horizontally at $8$ m/s. What is the rain's speed and angle from the vertical as seen by the driver?</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) Aim straight across: crossing velocity $5$ m/s, $t=60/5=12$ s, drift $=3(12)=36$ m, ground speed $\sqrt{25+9}=5.83$ m/s. To land opposite: cancel the current, $5\sin\alpha=3\Rightarrow\alpha=36.9^\circ$ upstream of straight across; crossing speed $5\cos\alpha=4$ m/s, $t=60/4=15$ s (longer).</p>
<p>(b) $\vec v_{rain/car}=\vec v_{rain}-\vec v_{car}=(-8,-6)$ m/s: speed $10$ m/s, tilted $\arctan(8/6)=53.1^\circ$ from the vertical toward the front of the car (the drops appear to come from ahead).</p>`},
  {id:"x12", title:"Monkey and hunter", problem:String.raw`<p>A monkey hangs $40$ m horizontally from a launcher and $30$ m above it. At the instant a dart is fired directly at the monkey with speed $50$ m/s, the monkey lets go. Use $g=10$ m/s². Show that the dart hits it, and find when and at what height above the launcher (ignore the ground).</p>`,
   solution:String.raw`<p>Aim direction: $\tan\theta=30/40$, so $v_{0x}=40$, $v_{0y}=30$ m/s. Dart: $x=40t$, $y=30t-5t^2$. Monkey: $x=40$, $y=30-5t^2$. The monkey's $y$ equals the dart's $y$ exactly when $30t=30$, i.e. $t=1$ s, at which the dart is also at $x=40t=40$ m. They meet at $t=1$ s at height $30-5=25$ m above the launcher. Both have fallen the same $\tfrac12gt^2$ below the straight line of sight, so the dart hits whatever the dart's speed, as long as it arrives before the ground (here, as long as the monkey has not fallen 30 m, $t&lt;\sqrt6=2.45$ s).</p>`}
  ],
  mcq:[
  {id:"q1", q:String.raw`<p>A particle's position is $x(t)=t^3-6t^2+9t$ (SI). The total distance it travels from $t=0$ to $t=4$ s is</p>`, choices:["4 m","8 m","12 m","16 m","20 m"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>$v=3(t-1)(t-3)$ changes sign at 1 s and 3 s. $x$ goes $0\to4\to0\to4$, so distance $=4+4+4=12$ m while displacement is only 4 m.</p>`},
  {id:"q2", q:String.raw`<p>A particle has $a(t)=6t$ (SI), starts with $v=2$ m/s at $x=0$. Its position at $t=3$ s is</p>`, choices:["21 m","27 m","29 m","33 m","45 m"], a:3,
   why:String.raw`<p>$v=2+3t^2$, so $x=2t+t^3$. At $t=3$: $6+27=33$ m. Using $x=v_0t+\tfrac12at^2$ would be wrong since $a$ is not constant.</p>`},
  {id:"q3", q:String.raw`<p>A ball is launched from level ground at 20 m/s at $30^\circ$ above horizontal. Taking $g=10$ m/s² and neglecting air resistance, the horizontal range is closest to</p>`, choices:["17 m","20 m","35 m","40 m","69 m"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>$R=v_0^2\sin2\theta/g=400(0.866)/10\approx34.6$ m.</p>`},
  {id:"q4", q:String.raw`<p>A ball is thrown straight up. At the very top of its flight, ignoring air resistance, its velocity and acceleration are</p>`, choices:["$v=0$ and $a=0$","$v=0$ and $a=g$ downward","$v$ upward and $a=0$","$v$ downward and $a=g$ downward","$v=0$ and $a$ upward"], a:1,
   why:String.raw`<p>The velocity is momentarily zero, but gravity keeps acting: $a=g$ downward the whole time. Zero velocity does not mean zero acceleration.</p>`},
  {id:"q5", q:String.raw`<p>An object moves along a line with $a=-kv$, where $k>0$, and initial speed $v_0$. The total distance it travels before stopping is</p>`, choices:["$v_0/k$","$v_0^2/2k$","$kv_0$","$v_0\\ln2/k$","infinite"], a:0,
   why:String.raw`<p>$v=v_0e^{-kt}$, so $x=\int_0^\infty v_0e^{-kt}dt=v_0/k$. (Alternatively $a=v\,dv/dx=-kv$ gives $dv/dx=-k$, $\Delta x=v_0/k$.) Check units: $k$ is 1/s, so only $v_0/k$ has dimensions of length. Note the distinction: the <i>distance</i> is finite, but the <i>time</i> to stop is infinite (the speed only approaches zero), so "infinite" is true of the time, not of the distance.</p>`},
  {id:"q6", q:String.raw`<p>Ball A is dropped from rest from a height $h$. At the same instant ball B is launched horizontally from the same height. Neglecting air resistance, which is true?</p>`, choices:["A lands first","B lands first","They land at the same time","The one with larger speed lands first","It depends on B's launch speed"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>The vertical motion is identical (zero initial vertical velocity, same $g$, same $h$). Horizontal velocity does not affect the fall time.</p>`},
  {id:"q7", q:String.raw`<p>A particle moves along $x$ with $v^2=4x$ (SI) for $x>0$. Its acceleration is</p>`, choices:["$1$ m/s²","$2$ m/s²","$4$ m/s²","$2\\sqrt{x}$ m/s²","not constant"], a:1,
   why:String.raw`<p>$a=v\,dv/dx=\tfrac12\,d(v^2)/dx=\tfrac12(4)=2$ m/s², constant.</p>`},
  {id:"q8", q:String.raw`<p>A car speeds up uniformly from 10 m/s to 30 m/s while traveling 100 m. How long does this take?</p>`, choices:["2.5 s","4 s","5 s","8 s","10 s"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>Average speed $=20$ m/s so $t=100/20=5$ s. (Equivalently $a=(30^2-10^2)/(2\cdot100)=4$ m/s², $t=20/4=5$ s.)</p>`},
  {id:"q9", q:String.raw`<p>A ball is launched from level ground at $v_0=10$ m/s and $45^\circ$ above the horizontal ($g=10$ m/s²). Using the trajectory $y=x\tan\theta-\dfrac{gx^2}{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta}$, its height when it is $5$ m from the launch point (horizontally) is</p>`, choices:["1.25 m","2.5 m","3.75 m","5 m","7.5 m"], a:1,
   why:String.raw`<p>$y=5-\dfrac{10(25)}{2(100)(0.5)}=5-2.5=2.5$ m. This is also the apex, since $R=v_0^2\sin90^\circ/g=10$ m and the apex is at $R/2=5$ m. The tempting 5 m is $x\tan\theta$ alone, which forgets that gravity bends the path.</p>`},
  {id:"q10", q:String.raw`<p>A ball is thrown horizontally at $12$ m/s from a $45$ m cliff ($g=10$ m/s², no air resistance). The speed with which it hits the ground is closest to</p>`, choices:["12 m/s","30 m/s","32 m/s","42 m/s","45 m/s"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>$t=\sqrt{2h/g}=3$ s, $v_y=30$ m/s, and $v=\sqrt{12^2+30^2}=32.3$ m/s (equivalently $\sqrt{v_0^2+2gh}=\sqrt{144+900}$). Adding the components, $12+30=42$, is the tempting error: velocity components add as vectors, in quadrature.</p>`},
  {id:"q11", q:String.raw`<p>Ball A is launched from level ground at $20^\circ$ above the horizontal and ball B at $70^\circ$, both at the same speed. Neglecting air resistance, compared with A, ball B has</p>`, choices:["the same range, a greater maximum height and a longer flight time","a shorter range, a greater maximum height and a longer flight time","the same range and the same maximum height","a longer range because it goes higher","the same range and the same flight time"], a:0,
   why:String.raw`<p>$R\propto\sin2\theta$ and $\sin40^\circ=\sin140^\circ$, so the ranges are equal (complementary angles). But $H\propto\sin^2\theta$ and $T\propto\sin\theta$ are both larger at $70^\circ$. Equal range does not mean equal flight.</p>`},
  {id:"q12", q:String.raw`<p>A projectile is launched with fixed speed from the edge of a cliff and lands on the level ground far below. The launch angle (above the horizontal) that gives the greatest horizontal distance is</p>`, choices:[String.raw`exactly $45^\circ$`,String.raw`greater than $45^\circ$`,String.raw`$0^\circ$ (horizontal)`,String.raw`$90^\circ$`,String.raw`less than $45^\circ$ but greater than $0^\circ$`], a:4,
   why:String.raw`<p>The best angle satisfies $\tan\theta=v_0/\sqrt{v_0^2+2gh}$, which is below 1 whenever $h&gt;0$: the extra height already supplies flight time, so a flatter launch converts more of $v_0$ into horizontal motion. It is still greater than $0^\circ$ because some upward velocity adds time. $45^\circ$ is the answer only for launch and landing at the same level.</p>`},
  {id:"q13", q:String.raw`<p>Three identical balls are thrown from the same cliff top with the same speed: one $30^\circ$ above the horizontal, one horizontally, one $30^\circ$ below. Neglecting air resistance, their speeds when they reach the ground below compare as</p>`, choices:["all three are equal","the ball thrown upward is fastest","the ball thrown downward is fastest","the horizontal ball is fastest","the horizontal ball is slowest, the other two equal"], a:0,
   why:String.raw`<p>$v=\sqrt{v_0^2+2gh}$ does not depend on direction (energy conservation, or $v\,dv=-g\,dy$). The upward ball has a longer flight, but it gains and loses the same speed on the way up and back to cliff height. The impact <i>angles</i> differ, and the arrival times differ, but not the speed.</p>`},
  {id:"q14", q:String.raw`<p>The graph shows the velocity of a particle on a line. Its displacement from $t=0$ to $t=8$ s is</p><svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 330 170" width="330" role="img" aria-label="v versus t: rises linearly from 0 at t=0 to 4 m/s at 2 s, flat at 4 m/s until 4 s, then falls linearly crossing zero at 6 s to minus 4 m/s at 8 s"><line x1="40" y1="90" x2="300" y2="90" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="20" x2="40" y2="160" stroke="currentColor"/><polyline points="40,90 100,42 160,42 280,138" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="22" y="46">4</text><text x="14" y="142">-4</text><text x="96" y="106">2</text><text x="156" y="106">4</text><text x="216" y="106">6</text><text x="276" y="106">8</text><text x="22" y="22">v (m/s)</text><text x="300" y="82">t (s)</text></svg>`, choices:["4 m","8 m","12 m","16 m","20 m"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>Signed areas: $0$ to $2$ s triangle $=4$; $2$ to $4$ s rectangle $=8$; $4$ to $6$ s triangle $=+4$; $6$ to $8$ s triangle $=-4$. Sum $=12$ m. The distance traveled would be $20$ m, which counts the last area as positive; displacement uses the sign.</p>`},
  {id:"q15", q:String.raw`<p>The graph shows position versus time for a particle on a line. During which interval is its speed greatest?</p><svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 330 180" width="330" role="img" aria-label="x versus t made of straight segments: from (0,0) to (2 s, 8 m), flat to 4 s, up to (6 s, 20 m), then down to (8 s, 14 m)"><line x1="40" y1="150" x2="300" y2="150" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="20" x2="40" y2="160" stroke="currentColor"/><polyline points="40,150 100,110 160,110 220,50 280,80" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="96" y="167">2</text><text x="156" y="167">4</text><text x="216" y="167">6</text><text x="276" y="167">8</text><text x="14" y="54">20</text><text x="22" y="114">8</text><text x="22" y="22">x (m)</text><text x="300" y="167">t (s)</text></svg>`, choices:["0 to 2 s","2 to 4 s","4 to 6 s","6 to 8 s","the speed is the same in every interval except 2 to 4 s"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>Speed is the magnitude of the slope of $x(t)$: $4$ m/s on 0 to 2 s, $0$ on 2 to 4 s, $6$ m/s on 4 to 6 s, $3$ m/s on 6 to 8 s (moving backward). The particle is highest at 6 s, but position is not speed: the tempting answer is the interval that ends at the highest point.</p>`},
  {id:"q16", q:String.raw`<p>A particle moves on a line with initial velocity $+2$ m/s at $t=0$. Its acceleration is shown. Its velocity at $t=7$ s is</p><svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 330 170" width="330" role="img" aria-label="a versus t: 2 m/s squared from 0 to 3 s, zero from 3 to 5 s, minus 4 m/s squared from 5 to 7 s"><line x1="40" y1="90" x2="300" y2="90" stroke="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="20" x2="40" y2="160" stroke="currentColor"/><polyline points="40,90 40,66 130,66 130,90 190,90 190,138 250,138 250,90" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="22" y="70">2</text><text x="14" y="142">-4</text><text x="126" y="106">3</text><text x="186" y="80">5</text><text x="246" y="80">7</text><text x="22" y="22">a (m/s²)</text><text x="300" y="82">t (s)</text></svg>`, choices:["$-8$ m/s","$-2$ m/s","$0$","$+8$ m/s","$+16$ m/s"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>$\Delta v$ is the area under $a(t)$: $2(3)=+6$ and $-4(2)=-8$, total $-2$. So $v(7)=2+(-2)=0$. The tempting $+8$ m/s is $v$ at 5 s (the maximum), stopping the accumulation before the negative area.</p>`},
  {id:"q17", q:String.raw`<p>A particle has position $\vec r(t)=3t^2\,\hat\imath+4t\,\hat\jmath$ (SI units). Its speed at $t=1$ s is</p>`, choices:["5.0 m/s","7.2 m/s","8.0 m/s","10 m/s","14 m/s"], a:1,
   why:String.raw`<p>$\vec v=6t\,\hat\imath+4\,\hat\jmath=(6,4)$ at $t=1$, so $|\vec v|=\sqrt{36+16}=7.2$ m/s. Adding components ($6+4=10$) ignores that they are perpendicular.</p>`},
  {id:"q18", q:String.raw`<p>At one instant a particle has velocity $\vec v=(3,4)$ m/s and acceleration $\vec a=(-4,3)$ m/s². At that instant the particle is</p>`, choices:["speeding up","slowing down","moving at constant speed momentarily, while its direction of motion changes","moving in a straight line","at rest"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>$\vec a\cdot\vec v=-12+12=0$, so $a_\parallel=0$ and the speed is not changing at that instant, but $\vec a\neq0$ is perpendicular to $\vec v$ and turns the velocity. A nonzero acceleration does not mean the speed is changing.</p>`},
  {id:"q19", q:String.raw`<p>A particle has $\vec r(t)=R(\cos\omega t\,\hat\imath+\sin\omega t\,\hat\jmath)$ with $R=0.5$ m and $\omega=4$ rad/s. The magnitude of its acceleration is</p>`, choices:["0","2 m/s²","4 m/s²","8 m/s²","16 m/s²"], a:3,
   why:String.raw`<p>$\vec a=-\omega^2\vec r$, so $|\vec a|=\omega^2R=16(0.5)=8$ m/s² (equal to $v^2/R=2^2/0.5$). The speed is constant at $2$ m/s, but the direction changes, so the acceleration is not zero.</p>`},
  {id:"q20", q:String.raw`<p>A swimmer heads straight across a $50$ m wide river at $1$ m/s relative to the water. The river flows at $2$ m/s. How far downstream does the swimmer land, measured along the bank?</p>`, choices:["25 m","50 m","100 m","112 m","150 m"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>Crossing time $=50/1=50$ s (only the across component matters), drift $=2(50)=100$ m. The $112$ m is the length of the diagonal path, not the distance along the bank.</p>`},
  {id:"q21", q:String.raw`<p>Two balls are launched from the same point at the same instant with different velocity vectors. Neglecting air resistance, the distance between them (before either lands)</p>`, choices:["grows linearly with time","grows quadratically with time","stays constant","grows and then shrinks","depends on $g$"], a:0,
   why:String.raw`<p>$\vec r_2-\vec r_1=(\vec v_{02}-\vec v_{01})t$: the $-\tfrac12gt^2$ terms cancel, so relative to each other the balls move in a straight line at constant velocity. The distance grows linearly and does not depend on $g$.</p>`},
  {id:"q22", q:String.raw`<p>A car moving at a constant $10$ m/s passes a point at the instant a second car starts from rest at that point with constant acceleration $2$ m/s². The second car catches the first after</p>`, choices:["2.5 s","5 s","7.5 s","10 s","20 s"], a:3,
   why:String.raw`<p>$10t=\tfrac12(2)t^2=t^2\Rightarrow t=10$ s, at $100$ m. At $5$ s the speeds are equal ($v=2t=10$) but that is when the gap is greatest ($25$ m), not when the cars meet.</p>`},
  {id:"q23", q:String.raw`<p>A glider moves along a line with $a=-kv^2$ ($k>0$) and initial speed $v_0$. Which statement is correct?</p>`, choices:[String.raw`It stops after traveling $v_0/k$.`,String.raw`It stops after traveling $v_0^2/2k$.`,String.raw`It stops after traveling $\ln2/k$.`,String.raw`Its speed never reaches zero and its distance traveled is unbounded.`,String.raw`It stops after a time $1/(kv_0)$.`], a:3,
   why:String.raw`<p>$a=v\,dv/dx=-kv^2\Rightarrow v=v_0e^{-kx}$, which is positive for every finite $x$; also $v=v_0/(1+kv_0t)$ never reaches zero. In contrast with $a=-kv$, where the stopping distance $v_0/k$ is finite (although the time is infinite). The time $1/(kv_0)$ is the time at which the speed has fallen to $v_0/2$, not to zero.</p>`},
  {id:"q24", q:String.raw`<p>A balloon is rising at $10$ m/s when it is $75$ m above the ground. A ball is dropped (released from rest relative to the balloon) at that instant. Use $g=10$ m/s². The ball reaches the ground after</p>`, choices:["3.9 s","5.0 s","6.0 s","7.5 s","8.0 s"], a:1,
   why:String.raw`<p>The ball starts with the balloon's velocity, $+10$ m/s upward: $0=75+10t-5t^2\Rightarrow t^2-2t-15=0\Rightarrow t=5$ s. The tempting $3.9$ s $=\sqrt{2(75)/10}$ treats the ball as if it started from rest in the ground frame.</p>`},
  {id:"q25", q:String.raw`<p>A ball is launched with speed $10$ m/s <i>perpendicular</i> to the surface of a $30^\circ$ incline ($g=10$ m/s²). The time until it returns to the incline is</p>`, choices:["1.15 s","2.00 s","2.31 s","3.46 s","4.00 s"], a:2,
   why:String.raw`<p>In tilted axes the perpendicular acceleration is $-g\cos30^\circ=-8.66$ m/s²: $T=2v_0/(g\cos\varphi)=20/8.66=2.31$ s. The tempting $2.00$ s uses the full $g$ ($T=2v_0/g$), as if the incline were horizontal.</p>`},
  {id:"q26", q:String.raw`<p>A runner covers the first $2$ km of a course at $4$ m/s and the next $2$ km at $6$ m/s. The average speed for the whole $4$ km is</p>`, choices:["4.0 m/s","4.8 m/s","5.0 m/s","5.2 m/s","6.0 m/s"], a:1,
   why:String.raw`<p>Total time $=2000/4+2000/6=500+333=833$ s, so the average speed is $4000/833=4.8$ m/s. The arithmetic mean $5.0$ m/s would be right only if the <i>times</i> were equal. Average speed is total distance over total time, not the mean of the speeds.</p>`}
  ],
  frq:[
  {id:"f1", type:"Mathematical routines", title:"Drag on a sliding puck",
   prompt:String.raw`<p>A puck of mass $m$ slides on a horizontal surface. Because of a thin fluid layer its acceleration is $a=-kv$, where $k$ is a positive constant. At $t=0$ the puck is at $x=0$ moving with velocity $v_0$ in the $+x$ direction.</p>
<p>(a) Derive $v(t)$.<br>(b) Derive $x(t)$.<br>(c) Determine the total distance the puck slides.<br>(d) Determine the time at which the speed has fallen to $v_0/2$.<br>(e) Sketch $v$ versus $t$ and $x$ versus $t$ on separate axes, labeling any intercepts and asymptotes.</p>`,
   pts:[
    {p:1,d:"Separates variables ($dv/v=-k\\,dt$) or states an equivalent correct differential equation"},
    {p:1,d:"Integrates with correct limits or initial condition, gets $v=v_0e^{-kt}$"},
    {p:1,d:"Integrates $v(t)$ for $x$ and applies $x(0)=0$"},
    {p:1,d:"$x(t)=\\frac{v_0}{k}(1-e^{-kt})$"},
    {p:1,d:"Stopping distance $v_0/k$ (limit as $t\\to\\infty$)"},
    {p:1,d:"$t=\\ln2/k$ with consistent work"},
    {p:1,d:"Sketches: $v$ decays from $v_0$ to a horizontal asymptote at 0; $x$ rises from 0 toward asymptote $v_0/k$, concave down"}],
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) $\dfrac{dv}{dt}=-kv\Rightarrow\int_{v_0}^v\dfrac{dv'}{v'}=-k\int_0^tdt'\Rightarrow \ln\dfrac{v}{v_0}=-kt\Rightarrow v=v_0e^{-kt}$.</p>
<p>(b) $x=\int_0^tv_0e^{-kt'}dt'=\dfrac{v_0}{k}\left(1-e^{-kt}\right)$.</p>
<p>(c) As $t\to\infty$, $x\to v_0/k$.</p>
<p>(d) $e^{-kt}=\tfrac12\Rightarrow t=\ln2/k$.</p>
<p>(e) $v(t)$: starts at $v_0$, exponential decay, asymptote $v=0$. $x(t)$: starts at 0 with slope $v_0$, concave down, asymptote $x=v_0/k$.</p>
<p class="mut">Check units: $k$ has units 1/s, so $v_0/k$ is a length. Always do this.</p>`},
  {id:"f2", type:"Mathematical routines", title:"Projectile launched from a cliff",
   prompt:String.raw`<p>A ball is launched from the top of a cliff of height $h$ with speed $v_0$ at an angle $\theta$ above the horizontal. Neglect air resistance. Let the origin be at the base of the cliff directly below the launch point.</p>
<p>(a) Derive the equation $y(x)$ for the path of the ball in terms of $h$, $v_0$, $\theta$ and $g$.<br>(b) For $v_0=10$ m/s, $\tan\theta=3/4$ ($\cos\theta=0.8$, $\sin\theta=0.6$), $h=8$ m and $g=10$ m/s², find the time of flight and the horizontal distance at which the ball lands.<br>(c) Find the speed of the ball just before it lands.<br>(d) Find the angle below the horizontal of its velocity at impact.<br>(e) The ball is instead launched horizontally from the same cliff at the same speed $v_0=10$ m/s. How does the impact speed compare with part (c)? Is the impact angle larger or smaller than in part (d)? Is the range larger or smaller? Justify without a full calculation.</p>`,
   pts:[
    {p:1,d:"Uses $x=v_0\\cos\\theta\\,t$ to write $t=x/(v_0\\cos\\theta)$ (or equivalent elimination)"},
    {p:1,d:"$y=h+x\\tan\\theta-\\dfrac{gx^2}{2v_0^2\\cos^2\\theta}$"},
    {p:1,d:"Sets $y=0$ with $v_{0y}=6$, $v_{0x}=8$: $0=8+6t-5t^2$"},
    {p:1,d:"$t=2$ s (rejects the negative root) and $x=16$ m"},
    {p:1,d:"$v_y=6-10(2)=-14$ m/s (or energy: $v^2=v_0^2+2gh$)"},
    {p:1,d:"$v=\\sqrt{64+196}=\\sqrt{260}\\approx16.1$ m/s"},
    {p:1,d:"$\\tan\\beta=14/8\\Rightarrow\\beta\\approx60^\\circ$ below the horizontal"},
    {p:1,d:"Same impact speed, since $v=\\sqrt{v_0^2+2gh}$ is independent of launch direction"},
    {p:1,d:"Impact angle smaller (shallower): $v_x$ is $10$ m/s instead of $8$ with the same speed (so $v_y$ is smaller); range is smaller since flight time is shorter (no upward initial velocity), $t=1.26$ s, range $12.6$ m"}],
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) $t=x/(v_0\cos\theta)$, so $y=h+v_0\sin\theta\,t-\tfrac12gt^2=h+x\tan\theta-\dfrac{gx^2}{2v_0^2\cos^2\theta}$.</p>
<p>(b) $0=8+6t-5t^2\Rightarrow5t^2-6t-8=0\Rightarrow(5t+4)(t-2)=0\Rightarrow t=2$ s. $x=8(2)=16$ m.</p>
<p>(c) $v_y=6-20=-14$ m/s, $v=\sqrt{8^2+14^2}=\sqrt{260}=16.1$ m/s (check: $\sqrt{100+2(10)(8)}=\sqrt{260}$).</p>
<p>(d) $\tan\beta=14/8=1.75$, $\beta=60.3^\circ$ below horizontal.</p>
<p>(e) The speed is the same, $16.1$ m/s, because it depends only on $v_0$ and $h$. With $v_x=10$ m/s (larger than 8) and the same speed, $|v_y|=\sqrt{260-100}=12.6$ m/s, so $\beta=\arctan(12.6/10)=51.7^\circ$, smaller. The horizontal launch flies for $\sqrt{2h/g}=1.26$ s versus 2 s, and covers $12.6$ m versus $16$ m.</p>`},
  {id:"f3", type:"Mathematical routines", title:"Range up an incline and the best angle",
   prompt:String.raw`<p>A ball is launched from a point on a long incline that makes a constant angle $\varphi$ with the horizontal. The launch speed is $v_0$ and the launch direction makes an angle $\alpha$ above the surface of the incline (the launch is up the slope, in the vertical plane that contains the line of steepest slope). Neglect air resistance.</p>
<p>(a) Using axes along and perpendicular to the incline, write the components of the acceleration of the ball.<br>(b) Derive the time of flight $T$ until the ball lands on the incline.<br>(c) Show that the distance $R$ along the incline at which it lands is $R=\dfrac{2v_0^2\sin\alpha\cos(\alpha+\varphi)}{g\cos^2\varphi}$.<br>(d) Find the angle $\alpha$ that maximizes $R$, and the maximum range, in terms of $v_0$, $g$ and $\varphi$.<br>(e) For $\varphi=30^\circ$ and $v_0=20$ m/s ($g=10$ m/s²), find the maximum range and the launch angle measured from the horizontal.<br>(f) If the launch were down the slope instead, would the maximum range be greater or less than $R_{max}$ in part (e)? Explain physically.</p>`,
   pts:[
    {p:1,d:"$a_{x'}=-g\\sin\\varphi$ along the slope, $a_{y'}=-g\\cos\\varphi$ perpendicular"},
    {p:1,d:"Sets $y'=0$: $T=2v_0\\sin\\alpha/(g\\cos\\varphi)$"},
    {p:1,d:"$R=v_0\\cos\\alpha\\,T-\\tfrac12g\\sin\\varphi\\,T^2$"},
    {p:1,d:"Simplifies with $\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\varphi-\\sin\\alpha\\sin\\varphi=\\cos(\\alpha+\\varphi)$ to the stated result"},
    {p:1,d:"Rewrites $\\sin\\alpha\\cos(\\alpha+\\varphi)=\\tfrac12[\\sin(2\\alpha+\\varphi)-\\sin\\varphi]$ or sets $dR/d\\alpha=0$"},
    {p:1,d:"$\\alpha=45^\\circ-\\varphi/2$ and $R_{max}=v_0^2/[g(1+\\sin\\varphi)]$"},
    {p:1,d:"$R_{max}=400/(10\\cdot1.5)=26.7$ m"},
    {p:1,d:"$\\alpha=30^\\circ$, so the angle from the horizontal is $\\alpha+\\varphi=60^\\circ$"},
    {p:1,d:"Greater: $R_{max}=v_0^2/[g(1-\\sin\\varphi)]=80$ m, since the ground falls away so the ball is in the air longer (and gravity's component along the slope helps instead of opposes)"}],
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) Along the slope (up positive) $a_{x'}=-g\sin\varphi$; perpendicular to it $a_{y'}=-g\cos\varphi$.</p>
<p>(b) $y'=v_0\sin\alpha\,t-\tfrac12g\cos\varphi\,t^2=0\Rightarrow T=\dfrac{2v_0\sin\alpha}{g\cos\varphi}$.</p>
<p>(c) $R=v_0\cos\alpha\,T-\tfrac12g\sin\varphi\,T^2=\dfrac{2v_0^2\sin\alpha}{g\cos\varphi}\left[\cos\alpha-\dfrac{\sin\varphi\sin\alpha}{\cos\varphi}\right]=\dfrac{2v_0^2\sin\alpha\cos(\alpha+\varphi)}{g\cos^2\varphi}$.</p>
<p>(d) $R=\dfrac{v_0^2}{g\cos^2\varphi}[\sin(2\alpha+\varphi)-\sin\varphi]$ is largest when $2\alpha+\varphi=90^\circ$: $\alpha=45^\circ-\varphi/2$, $R_{max}=\dfrac{v_0^2(1-\sin\varphi)}{g\cos^2\varphi}=\dfrac{v_0^2}{g(1+\sin\varphi)}$.</p>
<p>(e) $R_{max}=400/(10\times1.5)=26.7$ m at $\alpha=30^\circ$, i.e. $60^\circ$ above the horizontal.</p>
<p>(f) Down the slope $\varphi\to-\varphi$: $R_{max}=v_0^2/[g(1-\sin\varphi)]=400/5=80$ m. Greater: the landing surface falls away from the ball so it flies longer.</p>`},
  {id:"f4", type:"Experimental design & analysis", title:"Measuring a launch speed from range data",
   prompt:String.raw`<p>A student wants to determine the speed $v_0$ with which a small steel ball leaves the end of a horizontal track, and to test whether the motion after leaving the track is consistent with projectile motion without air resistance. The track is clamped to a table whose height can be varied. The student releases the ball from the same mark on the track every time, which makes $v_0$ the same for every trial.</p>
<p>(a) Describe the measurements, the equipment, and a procedure the student should use (including how to reduce random error).<br>(b) Derive an expression for the horizontal distance $x$ at which the ball lands on the floor in terms of $v_0$, the launch height $H$ above the floor and $g$. State what quantities should be plotted to produce a straight line, and what the slope represents.<br>(c) The student's data are in the table. Plot $x^2$ versus $H$ (or compute it) and use the data to determine the slope and $v_0$. Use $g=9.8$ m/s².</p>
<table><tr><th>$H$ (m)</th><td>0.20</td><td>0.40</td><td>0.60</td><td>0.80</td><td>1.00</td></tr><tr><th>$x$ (m)</th><td>0.40</td><td>0.57</td><td>0.70</td><td>0.80</td><td>0.90</td></tr></table>
<p>(d) The best-fit line has a $y$-intercept very close to zero. Explain why this is expected, and what a clearly positive or negative intercept would suggest about the measurement of $H$.<br>(e) Air resistance is not actually negligible. How would it change the measured slope and the value of $v_0$ the student calculates? Explain.<br>(f) Use the best-fit line to predict $x$ for $H=1.50$ m.</p>`,
   pts:[
    {p:1,d:"Measures $x$ (meter stick or tape, with a marked landing spot, e.g. carbon paper) and $H$ (meter stick, from the ball's launch height to the floor)"},
    {p:1,d:"Procedure: vary $H$, release from the same mark, repeat each height several times and average $x$ (random error)"},
    {p:1,d:"$H=\\tfrac12gt^2$, $x=v_0t$, so $x=v_0\\sqrt{2H/g}$"},
    {p:1,d:"Plots $x^2$ versus $H$ (linear), slope $=2v_0^2/g$"},
    {p:1,d:"Slope from the data $\\approx0.81$ m (any value $0.79$ to $0.83$ m accepted)"},
    {p:1,d:"$v_0=\\sqrt{g\\cdot\\text{slope}/2}\\approx2.0$ m/s"},
    {p:1,d:"Intercept zero: $x=0$ when $H=0$; a nonzero intercept suggests an offset in $H$ (for example measuring to the ball's bottom or top instead of its center)"},
    {p:1,d:"Air resistance reduces $x$, so slope and the calculated $v_0$ are too small (the ball's real launch speed is larger than computed); the effect grows with $H$ and so makes the data curve below the line"},
    {p:1,d:"$x^2\\approx0.81(1.50)=1.21$, $x\\approx1.10$ m"}],
   solution:String.raw`<p>(a) Measure the launch height $H$ (vertical distance from the ball's center at the track's end to the floor) and the landing distance $x$ (from a plumb line below the end of the track). Mark landing spots with carbon paper on white paper. Change $H$ by changing the table height; release the ball from the same mark each time; repeat 5 to 10 times at each height and average $x$ to reduce random error.</p>
<p>(b) Vertical: $H=\tfrac12gt^2\Rightarrow t=\sqrt{2H/g}$; horizontal: $x=v_0t=v_0\sqrt{2H/g}$. So $x^2=\dfrac{2v_0^2}{g}H$: plot $x^2$ (vertical axis) versus $H$; the slope is $2v_0^2/g$.</p>
<p>(c) $x^2=0.160,\,0.325,\,0.490,\,0.640,\,0.810$ m². The best-fit slope is $\approx0.81$ m (check with the end points: $(0.810-0.160)/(1.00-0.20)=0.81$ m). Then $v_0=\sqrt{g\,\text{slope}/2}=\sqrt{9.8(0.81)/2}=1.99\approx2.0$ m/s.</p>
<p>(d) At $H=0$ the ball is already at the floor, so $x=0$. A positive or negative intercept means every $H$ is off by a constant, e.g. $H$ measured from the wrong reference point.</p>
<p>(e) Drag slows the horizontal motion, so $x$ is smaller than the ideal value, the slope is lowered and the inferred $v_0=\sqrt{g\,\text{slope}/2}$ is lower than the true launch speed. The longer flights (larger $H$) lose more, so the points bend below the line.</p>
<p>(f) $x^2=0.81(1.50)=1.21$ m², $x=1.10$ m.</p>`}
  ]
});
